ance uh  Belgium and Holland and only then would  we begin to resort to blackouts but that  would also be accompanied by firing up  three  um coal burning stations which were due  to be mothballed getting them back into  service to cover some of the shortfall  and then we'll be looking at Power cuts  which would be carefully rolled out and  calibrated  um among the population with their  protection so we're told for people who  are vulnerable so I think that to think  that we're going to have uncontrolled  power Cuts occurring at any time of the  day without warning like some other poor  countries in in poor parts of the world  I think is is incorrect it is a serious  situation but let's not get panicking to  uh to to too early but the guardian is  looking at the worst case scenario and  we've got people uh predicting what  could possibly happening they're  suggesting loss of life if those who  need life-saving machines are having to  turn them off for three hours a day so  although what you seem to be saying is  that it's a bit of scaremongering  perhaps this is the likely outcome if we  do get to that that end stage Jenny  well I mean I do think this is a bit  alarmist as well because of course  hospitals are not going to be affected  the guardian's coverage is about people  who require life-saving medical  equipment at home so we're talking about  things like dialysis and uh ventilators  they simply cannot be without  electricity and the guardian does make  the point that the choice that they are  going to be faced with people who rely  on that equipment is whether or not to  go into hospital and be admitted to  hospital which is not a choice you would  want to make certainly if you're a  parent of a child that needs a  ventilator or needs any of this  life-saving equipment you wouldn't want  them to be in hospital when they could  be at home but it doesn't necessarily  mean that lives are going to be at risk  and what this story seems to be is the  worst case sc